.TH CORE 5 "November 25, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
core \- format of memory image file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/param.h>
.SH DESCRIPTION
The UNIX System writes out a memory image of a terminated
process when any of various errors occur.  See
.IR sigvec (2)
for the list of reasons; the most common are memory violations, illegal
instructions, bus errors, and user-generated quit signals.
The memory image is called
.IR prog .core,
.I prog
being the name of the program, and is written in the process's
working directory (provided it can be; normal access controls apply).
.PP
On the 80286 the core file consists of the
.I u.
area, USIZE pages of 512 bytes (UBYTES, in
.RI < sys/param.h >).
The
.I u.
area starts with a
.I user
structure as given in
.RI < sys/user.h >,
which holds the 80287's registers in
.IR u_fps .
The rest of the
.I u.
area is the kernel stack for the terminated process, at whose top is
the trap frame, with the processor's registers at the time of the fault
.RI ( struct
.I trapframe
in
.RI < machine/frame.h >).
.PP
Next is the process's table of segments, NSEG
.I pseg
structures as given in
.RI < machine/seg.h >.
Slot
.I n
of the table is the segment whose selector is 8\fIn\fP+7 in the process.
Last are the contents of each segment in the table that is in use
(PS_USED) and is not the program's shared code (PS_TEXT), in the order
of the table, each
.I ps_limit
+ 1 bytes.
The shared code is not in the core file: it is the same as in the
program.
.PP
In general the debugger
.IR adb (1)
is sufficient to deal with core images.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
adb(1), sigvec(2), stack(5)
